 ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
 :the-one-mismatch
 {:layer "as-int"
  :input "12abc"
  :native 0
  :nbb-oracle 12
  :verdict :oracle-disagrees-with-itself
  :explanation
  "aadhaar.main/as-int is a reader conditional. The :clj arm is
   (Long/parseLong (str/trim v)) inside a try whose catch answers 0, so on the
   JVM it answers 0. The :cljs arm is (js/parseInt v 10), which truncates at
   the first non-digit and answers 12. The port matches the :clj arm.
   This is not a defect introduced by the port -- it is a pre-existing
   platform split in the oracle that the port made visible."
  :measured "The :cljs arm, under nbb: js/parseInt gives 12. Confirmed."
  :not-measured
  "The :clj arm was NOT executed. This is a JVM-free exercise, so its value of
   0 is read from the source and from Long/parseLong's documented contract,
   not run. If that reading is wrong, the port matches neither arm."}

 ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
 :as-float
 ;; The blocked layer, and here -- unlike in the sibling -- it is reached.
 {:disposition :blocked
  :probe "q9-probes/g23-f64.kotoba"
  :check-exit 0
  :native-compile-exit 70
  :refusal
  "typed values currently require the kotoba-script web target, typed Wasm target, or qualified native string/scalar-record/option-i64/result-i64 features"

  :note-on-the-first-probe
  "A first attempt declared the return type :f64 directly and was refused by
   CHECK with a different message -- 'expression type mismatch: expected f64,
   got [:option :f64]'. That is the parser's failure being representable in the
   type, which is the language working, not the gap. The probe was corrected to
   thread the option through option-value-of, at which point check passes and
   the BACKEND is what refuses. Recording the first message as the gap would
   have been recording my own spelling error as a language finding."

  :why-it-matters-more-here-than-in-aave
  "aave: no spec coerces to :float, so as-float was missing but unreachable.
   aadhaar: Transaction is :coerce {:amount :float} and `amount` is one of its
   two REQUIRED fields. The arm is on the create and update path of one of the
   six entities."

  :what-the-port-does-instead
  "coerce-field carries an EXPLICIT `float` arm that preserves the raw JSON
   token, placed after the empty-token check so an absent field still answers
   null. It is written out rather than left to fall through :else, so the
   divergence is visible in the source instead of being an accident of cond
   ordering."

  :measured-divergence
  ;; What aadhaar.main stores vs what this port stores, per input.
  {"JSON number 12.5" {:oracle "12.5" :port "12.5" :agree true}
   "JSON string \"12.5\"" {:oracle "12.5" :port "\"12.5\"" :agree false}
   "JSON string \"abc\"" {:oracle "0" :port "\"abc\"" :agree false}
   "JSON number 0" {:oracle "0" :port "0" :agree true}
   "absent field" {:oracle "0" :port "null" :agree false}}

  :summary-of-the-divergence
  "The float arm agrees only when the incoming JSON token is already a decimal
   literal that round-trips as text. It diverges for a numeric STRING (the port
   keeps it a string where the oracle makes it a number), for non-numeric input
   (the oracle coerces to 0, the port keeps the text), and for an absent field
   (the oracle stores 0, the port stores null). Three of five probed inputs
   disagree."

  :consequence
  "This port is PARTIAL, not complete. One field of one of six entities does
   not store what aadhaar.main stores. That is recorded here rather than being
   rounded up, and the component is not reshaped to dodge it: the entity table
   still declares amount:float truthfully."}

